<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Carvetii</h1><p class="st-meta">50 CE – 410 CE (approx.) · capital Luguvalium (Carlisle)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of the Eden valley in Cumbria, known almost entirely from Roman inscriptions naming their self-governing civitas at Carlisle.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Carvetii lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-eden-cumbria">Eden</a> valley and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>. They are not named by any ancient historian, and they may originally have been a section of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/brigantes">Brigantes</a> that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> recognised as a separate community after the conquest of the north in the 70s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their existence is known from inscriptions: a 3rd-century milestone from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brougham">Brougham</a> names the civitas Carvetiorum, and a tombstone from Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penrith">Penrith</a> records a councillor of the Carvetii. These show a self-governing community with a council, administered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Luguvalium</a>, Carlisle, close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. The countryside was farmed by native communities living in enclosed settlements.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As one of the northernmost civitates of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>, the community lasted until Roman rule ended around 410. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> remained an important centre afterwards, and the area later formed part of British kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-rheged">Rheged</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/carvetii.jpg" alt="Carvetii" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: England_Celtic_tribes_-_North_and_Midlands.png: self-created Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Brigantes.svg: *England_Celt... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Carvetii.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 CE – 410 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luguvalium (Carlisle)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Romano-Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=230&amp;focus=carvetii" aria-label="Carvetii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4615 300 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Carvetii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4935 556L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4935 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4935 556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Luguvalium. <a href="/atlas?year=230&amp;focus=carvetii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

